"post-message " data-role=3D"message" dir=3D"auto"><div><p>Is this really t=
rue? Because this seems really, really non-intuitive.</p><p>If I make an or=
thographic frustum with range 0 (near) to 10 (far), I would expect that my =
object with z value 5.0 would show up as it's right in the middle of that r=
ange. That has not been the case, until I set my far plane to -10. This has=
 been confusing the hell out of me for quite some time. I've been trying to=
 figure out what the hell is going on as I've set everything up to be as si=
mple as possible. Thankfully I have read your comment.</p><p>What's strange=
 to me is that everyone keeps saying Z is positive from the screen to my ey=
es, which is what I'd like it to be. Everything in default openGL seems to =
imply the opposite. The default z buffer is cleared to a value of 1 (in a r=
ange from 0 to 1). Why would the default value of the depth buffer be the c=
losest possible value? It should be the furthest (0), unless you want every=
 item that's not as close as possible to fail the depth test. The default d=
epth test is LESS. In a system where lesser z values are supposed to be fur=
ther away, why would they pass the depth test? They are suppose to be occlu=
ded by the greater values. All these defaults make sense if you assume the =
Z values increase into the screen.</p><p>In order to actually get things wo=
rking intuitively, I must strangely set my glm ortho frustum to near =3D 0 =
and far =3D -10. I changed my depth clear value to 0 (the minimum), and cha=
nged the function to GL_GEQUAL. Now objects in a z range of 0 to 10 show up=
, and the objects of a greater or equal z value will appear above others.</=
p></div></div><span class=3D"post-media"><ul data-role=3D"post-media-list">=
