=3D"message-content"><div class=3D"post-message " data-role=3D"message" dir=
=3D"auto"><div><p><em>This has been confusing the hell out of me for quite =
some time. I've been trying to figure out what the hell is going on as I've=
 set everything up to be as simple as possible.</em></p><p>I agree that it'=
s all confusing, especially to visualize things. As the near/far plane are =
negated behind the scenes and the positive z-direction is <strong>towards</=
strong> the viewer (through the screen), you'd want to place your objects t=
owards the negative z-direction (in front of the virtual camera). However, =
this is confusing and most people build a camera system that (sort of) agai=
n negates the values such that it all makes much more sense. In the next tu=
torial we build such a system.</p><p><em>This has been confusing the hell o=
ut of me for quite some time. I've been trying to figure out what the hell =
is going on as I've set everything up to be as simple as possible.</em></p>=
<p>This is again confusing. If you use a projection matrix, all the values =
you supply when multiplying with this projection matrix assume the positive=
 Z direction is from the screen to your eyes. However, when the coordinates=
 are in normalized device coordinates (NDC) it is actually the opposite. Th=
e coordinates in NDC are in a left-handed coordinate system (positive z-dir=
ection is away from the viewer). But you usually don't really specify coord=
inates in NDC directly so there's no need to worry about it; but good to kn=
ow,.</p><p><em>Why would the default value of the depth buffer be the close=
st possible value?</em></p><p>I don't think you should think of the resulti=
ng depth value as nearest and furthest. The depth comparrison directly comp=
ares their depth values together and because the depth buffer is cleared to=
 <code>1.0</code>, all other depth values will most likely always be <code>=
LESS</code> (so there is likely to be some sort of negation again happening=
 there). If you want you can actually define a new depth value when resetti=
ng its content using <code>glClearDepth</code>.</p><p><em>In order to actua=
lly get things working intuitively, I must strangely set my glm ortho frust=
um to near =3D 0 and far =3D -10.</em></p><p>Work your way through the next=
 tutorial and get a View matrix set up. A view matrix basically negates the=
 translation of all objects: if you want to move to the right, all objects =
move to the left; and a similar trick happens with the z-direction. In that=
 case you can properly specify your object positions with regards to the pr=
ojection's frustum (at least it makes more sense that way).</p></div></div>=
