"publisher-anchor-color" data-role=3D"message-content"><div class=3D"post-m=
essage " data-role=3D"message" dir=3D"auto"><div><p>This is most likely bec=
ause of z-fighting. The depth buffer doesn't have infinite precision so whe=
n two objects have surfaces that are close or exactly aligned to each other=
, the depth buffer doesn't always have the precision to figure out which of=
 the two faces is the front face. This gives rise to artifacts where some s=
urfaces suddenly tend to pop over other surfaces or you see a bit of both i=
n a zig-zag like pattern. I briefly touch upon this at the end of the Advan=
ced-OpenGL/Depth-Testing tutorial. The best solution to these kind of probl=
ems is to basically prevent objects from having the exact same positions.</=
p><p>As for the 58 FPS. GLFW supports V-Sync which constrains the applicati=
on's FPS to your monitor's refresh rate (which is often ~60Hz). It doesn't =
seem to be enabled by default on all devices as on some devices I still get=
 high FPS numbers, while with other devices it also seems to be constrained=
 at around 58-60 FPS. You can probably disable it somewhere if it bothers y=
ou too much.</p></div></div><span class=3D"post-media"><ul data-role=3D"pos=
